, and context\n\nReliable scouting combines structured observation, context, repeated viewings, role-specific criteria, and data rather than relying on memorable highlights.\n\nClubs balance immediate performance, age profile, development pathways, contract risk, resale value, roster construction, and competitive windows.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nEvaluation can include role-specific performance, age-adjusted development, physical profile, availability, decision quality, contextual data, and how prior projections performed.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nScouts improve through calibration exercises, shared definitions, blind review, feedback on past decisions, and exposure to varied leagues and age groups.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nSafeguarding minors, anti-discrimination rules, consent, privacy, agent regulations, and transparent recruitment processes protect athletes and organisations.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nScouting and recruitment improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Scouting and recruitment: What beginners often misunderstand","word_count":589,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Scouting and recruitment"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIFA","title":"FIFA","url":"https://www.fifa.com/legal","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-188","published_at":"2026-08-03T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"scouting-and-recruitment-how-training-adapts-over-time-188","title":"Scouting and recruitment: How training adapts over time","description":"Scouting and recruitment explained through how training adapts over time, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Scouting and recruitment: How training adapts over time\n\nScouting and recruitment is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on how training adapts over time while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nScouting and recruitment identify athletes whose current ability, future development, tactical fit, character, availability, and cost match an organisation's needs.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## How training adapts over time\n\nScouts improve through calibration exercises, shared definitions, blind review, feedback on past decisions, and exposure to varied leagues and age groups. As an athlete improves, the same session creates a smaller stimulus, so training must change through load, complexity, speed, opposition, specificity, or recovery. Progress is not linear, and periods of consolidation are part of development rather than evidence of failure.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nReliable scouting combines structured observation, context, repeated viewings, role-specific criteria, and data rather than relying on memorable highlights.\n\nClubs balance immediate performance, age profile, development pathways, contract risk, resale value, roster construction, and competitive windows.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nEvaluation can include role-specific performance, age-adjusted development, physical profile, availability, decision quality, contextual data, and how prior projections performed.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nScouts improve through calibration exercises, shared definitions, blind review, feedback on past decisions, and exposure to varied leagues and age groups.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nSafeguarding minors, anti-discrimination rules, consent, privacy, agent regulations, and transparent recruitment processes protect athletes and organisations.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nScouting and recruitment improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-4.png","featured_image_alt":"Scouting and recruitment: How training adapts over time","word_count":599,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Scouting and recruitment"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIFA","title":"FIFA","url":"https://www.fifa.com/legal","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-189","published_at":"2026-08-04T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"scouting-and-recruitment-what-rules-and-safety-standards-protect-189","title":"Scouting and recruitment: What rules and safety standards protect","description":"Scouting and recruitment explained through what rules and safety standards protect, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Scouting and recruitment: What rules and safety standards protect\n\nScouting and recruitment is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on what rules and safety standards protect while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nScouting and recruitment identify athletes whose current ability, future development, tactical fit, character, availability, and cost match an organisation's needs.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## What rules and safety standards protect\n\nSafeguarding minors, anti-discrimination rules, consent, privacy, agent regulations, and transparent recruitment processes protect athletes and organisations. Rules do more than decide winners. They define acceptable contact, equipment, venue conditions, medical responsibilities, and procedural fairness. Strong safety systems combine prevention, trained supervision, emergency response, and a culture in which athletes can report problems without penalty.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nReliable scouting combines structured observation, context, repeated viewings, role-specific criteria, and data rather than relying on memorable highlights.\n\nClubs balance immediate performance, age profile, development pathways, contract risk, resale value, roster construction, and competitive windows.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nEvaluation can include role-specific performance, age-adjusted development, physical profile, availability, decision quality, contextual data, and how prior projections performed.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nScouts improve through calibration exercises, shared definitions, blind review, feedback on past decisions, and exposure to varied leagues and age groups.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nSafeguarding minors, anti-discrimination rules, consent, privacy, agent regulations, and transparent recruitment processes protect athletes and organisations.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nScouting and recruitment improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Scouting and recruitment: What rules and safety standards protect","word_count":595,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Scouting and recruitment"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"FIFA","title":"FIFA","url":"https://www.fifa.com/legal","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-190","published_at":"2026-08-05T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"coaching-communication-how-it-works-190","title":"Coaching communication: How it works","description":"Coaching communication explained through how it works, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Coaching communication: How it works\n\nCoaching communication is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on how it works while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nCoaching communication turns goals, observations, and feedback into cues athletes can understand and use under pressure.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## How it works\n\nCoaching communication turns goals, observations, and feedback into cues athletes can understand and use under pressure. The system works because individual actions are connected to a larger structure: technique produces options, decisions select among those options, and rules define which outcomes are legal. Coaches vary when to instruct, question, demonstrate, observe, or stay silent depending on whether the aim is learning, confidence, tactical adjustment, or autonomy.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nEffective cues are specific, timed appropriately, matched to the athlete's experience, and limited enough that attention can stay on the task.\n\nCoaches vary when to instruct, question, demonstrate, observe, or stay silent depending on whether the aim is learning, confidence, tactical adjustment, or autonomy.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nCommunication quality can be assessed through athlete understanding, recall, behavioural change, training flow, error correction, and feedback from athletes and staff.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nCoaches improve through video review, reflective practice, rehearsal, mentoring, and testing whether athletes can explain the intended objective in their own words.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nSafeguarding, professional boundaries, anti-harassment rules, confidentiality, and respect for athlete welfare constrain how authority should be exercised.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nCoaching communication improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Coaching communication: How it works","word_count":604,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Coaching communication"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"IOC","title":"IOC","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc/safeguarding","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-191","published_at":"2026-08-06T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"coaching-communication-why-technique-matters-191","title":"Coaching communication: Why technique matters","description":"Coaching communication explained through why technique matters, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Coaching communication: Why technique matters\n\nCoaching communication is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on why technique matters while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nCoaching communication turns goals, observations, and feedback into cues athletes can understand and use under pressure.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## Why technique matters\n\nEffective cues are specific, timed appropriately, matched to the athlete's experience, and limited enough that attention can stay on the task. Technique matters most when it remains reliable under speed, fatigue, uncertainty, and pressure. It is therefore better understood as repeatable problem-solving than as a perfectly fixed visual style.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nEffective cues are specific, timed appropriately, matched to the athlete's experience, and limited enough that attention can stay on the task.\n\nCoaches vary when to instruct, question, demonstrate, observe, or stay silent depending on whether the aim is learning, confidence, tactical adjustment, or autonomy.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nCommunication quality can be assessed through athlete understanding, recall, behavioural change, training flow, error correction, and feedback from athletes and staff.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nCoaches improve through video review, reflective practice, rehearsal, mentoring, and testing whether athletes can explain the intended objective in their own words.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nSafeguarding, professional boundaries, anti-harassment rules, confidentiality, and respect for athlete welfare constrain how authority should be exercised.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nCoaching communication improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Coaching communication: Why technique matters","word_count":587,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Coaching communication"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"IOC","title":"IOC","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc/safeguarding","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"IOC","title":"International Olympic Committee","url":"https://olympics.com/ioc","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WHO","title":"World Health Organization - Physical activity","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/physical-activity","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"sports-archive-2026-192","published_at":"2026-08-07T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"coaching-communication-where-strategy-changes-outcomes-192","title":"Coaching communication: Where strategy changes outcomes","description":"Coaching communication explained through where strategy changes outcomes, with technique, strategy, measurement, training progression, and safety context.","body_markdown":"# Coaching communication: Where strategy changes outcomes\n\nCoaching communication is best understood as a system of skill, decision-making, physical preparation, rules, and competitive context. This article focuses on where strategy changes outcomes while keeping the wider performance environment visible.\n\n## The performance system\n\nCoaching communication turns goals, observations, and feedback into cues athletes can understand and use under pressure.\n\nA useful way to analyse sport is to separate what the athlete can control directly from what is constrained by opponents, equipment, environment, and rules. Physical capacity creates options, technical skill makes those options usable, and decision-making determines which option is selected at a particular moment.\n\n## Where strategy changes outcomes\n\nCoaches vary when to instruct, question, demonstrate, observe, or stay silent depending on whether the aim is learning, confidence, tactical adjustment, or autonomy. Strategy matters because athletes and teams operate with limited time, energy, information, and risk tolerance. A good plan creates favourable probabilities, but it still depends on execution and adaptation when the opponent or environment changes.\n\n## Technique, tactics, and context\n\nEffective cues are specific, timed appropriately, matched to the athlete's experience, and limited enough that attention can stay on the task.\n\nCoaches vary when to instruct, question, demonstrate, observe, or stay silent depending on whether the aim is learning, confidence, tactical adjustment, or autonomy.\n\nThat distinction matters because two athletes can perform the same visible action for different reasons. Context changes the value of a decision. Score, time remaining, field position, fatigue, opponent behaviour, weather, equipment, competition format, and risk tolerance can all alter what the best choice looks like.\n\n## Measuring progress without oversimplifying it\n\nCommunication quality can be assessed through athlete understanding, recall, behavioural change, training flow, error correction, and feedback from athletes and staff.\n\nPerformance analysis should therefore combine outcome measures with process measures. Winning a possession, race, rally, or round is important, but coaches also need to know whether the underlying actions are becoming more repeatable. Good measurement reduces uncertainty without pretending that sport is fully predictable.\n\n## How training develops\n\nCoaches improve through video review, reflective practice, rehearsal, mentoring, and testing whether athletes can explain the intended objective in their own words.\n\nTraining should become more representative as skill improves. Early practice often simplifies the task so the athlete can learn a movement or rule. Later practice introduces pressure, variability, tactical choices, time constraints, fatigue, and opponents. The goal is transfer: the athlete should be able to use the skill in competition rather than only reproduce it in a drill.\n\nRecovery is part of that process. Adaptation occurs when training stress is followed by enough restoration for the athlete to absorb it. More work is not automatically better work, especially when technique deteriorates or fatigue begins to hide the quality coaches are trying to develop.\n\n## Rules, safety, and fair competition\n\nSafeguarding, professional boundaries, anti-harassment rules, confidentiality, and respect for athlete welfare constrain how authority should be exercised.\n\nSport also depends on integrity. Officials, governing bodies, coaches, event organisers, and athletes share responsibility for applying rules consistently, protecting participants, and maintaining conditions in which performance rather than avoidable hazard decides the outcome.\n\n## What to remember\n\nCoaching communication improves when athletes and coaches connect technique, tactical context, physical preparation, measurement, and recovery instead of treating them as separate subjects. The strongest training environments make objectives explicit, use rules and safety standards seriously, and evaluate whether practice is transferring to competition.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel sports explainer. Competition rules and technical regulations can change, so the relevant governing body's current rulebook should be checked when a decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/sports-fallback-4.png","featured_image_alt":"Coaching communication: Where strategy changes outcomes","word_count":599,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["sports","evergreen","explainer","Coaching communication"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null